Use native buttons, give each icon-only button an accessible name, and restore a visible keyboard focus indicator. Keep the icons small if you like, but make the buttons themselves larger.

```tsx
type IconToolbarProps = {
  onDelete: () => void;
  onShare: () => void;
};

export function IconToolbar({ onDelete, onShare }: IconToolbarProps) {
  return (
    <div className="toolbar" role="group" aria-label="Item actions">
      <button
        type="button"
        className="icon-btn"
        aria-label="Delete"
        onClick={onDelete}
      >
        <span aria-hidden="true"><TrashIcon /></span>
      </button>

      <button
        type="button"
        className="icon-btn"
        aria-label="Share"
        onClick={onShare}
      >
        <span aria-hidden="true"><ShareIcon /></span>
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Replace the existing `.icon-btn:focus { outline: none; }` rule with:

```css
.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 8px;
}

.icon-btn {
  display: inline-grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-inline-size: 44px;
  min-block-size: 44px;
}

.icon-btn:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid currentColor;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```

Native buttons support Tab navigation and Enter/Space activation without custom handlers. `type="button"` prevents accidental form submission, and `aria-hidden` keeps the decorative icons out of the accessibility tree. The labeled group keeps ordinary Tab navigation rather than implying the arrow-key behavior expected of an ARIA toolbar.

Use native buttons so both actions work with Tab, Enter, and Space. Give each button an accessible name, keep its icon decorative, and make the clickable area larger than the icon.

